&lt;h2&gt;&lt;strong&gt;About the team&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Avride builds autonomous driving technology for vehicles and delivery robots, and tests it on public roads in Austin. Before anything reaches those roads it goes through our QA team: checks on every pull request, a daily validation of main, and release-candidate qualification over a large set of recorded scenes. Every one of those gates produces a verdict, and the safety part of that verdict is the one people act on.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;&lt;strong&gt;About the role&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;You will own &lt;strong&gt;how safety is tested, judged and reported across our QA team.&lt;/strong&gt; Our Head of Safety owns the company&#39;s safety framework and the safety case built on top of it. You own the layer beneath: the gates, the criteria and the reports that turn day-to-day testing into evidence that argument can rest on.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The hard part of this is not the collisions. Those are obvious, and nobody argues about them. It is everything short of a collision&amp;nbsp; the near miss, the late brake, the hesitation at an intersection that looked wrong to one engineer and fine to another where the judgement has to be made and then made the same way again next week, by someone else, on a different run. Turning that into criteria a whole team applies consistently is the craft of this role.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;You will lead through process and through people who do not report to you.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What you’ll do&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Own the safety gates.&lt;/strong&gt; What counts as a safety-relevant finding, how it is classified and escalated, the criteria that hold a merge or a release, and how findings are tracked to closure&amp;nbsp; with the recurring ones feeding back into the criteria.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Own the acceptance criteria.&lt;/strong&gt; Decide what we measure at each gate from collisions and near misses to right-of-way and hard braking where the thresholds sit, and move them as the technology changes.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Own the safety report.&lt;/strong&gt; Define the format the whole QA team writes to: comparable between runs, explicit about what was not covered, and short enough to be read before a decision rather than after it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Coach the QA team.&lt;/strong&gt; Run calibration sessions, review reports, and teach the reasoning behind the criteria, so that safety judgement is something the team applies on its own rather than something you add at review.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Be the safety voice in the release decision.&lt;/strong&gt; Releases are decided by a review that brings together engineering, safety and operations. Your part is the one nobody else can supply: what the testing actually showed and what is still unknown&amp;nbsp; put plainly enough that the decision is made with its eyes open.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Connect hazards to evidence.&lt;/strong&gt; Take hazard analysis output&amp;nbsp; STPA, FMEA, HAZOP&amp;nbsp; through to what our scenario set has to contain, through to results, through to something our safety case can stand on.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Work with the safety organization.&lt;/strong&gt; Translate the safety framework into testable requirements with our Head of Safety, and work with development and analytics on the tooling that makes those requirements measurable.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Keep up with the rules that apply to us.&lt;/strong&gt; Track the standards and regulation governing driverless operation in Texas and beyond, and bring what matters into how we test.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What you&#39;ll need&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;7+ years in testing, verification or validation,&lt;/strong&gt; including ownership of something that outlived your involvement — a gate, a review, a reporting standard.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Experience where being wrong had physical consequences.&lt;/strong&gt; Autonomous vehicles, aviation, medical devices, rail, industrial automation, defence — the field matters far less than the habits it builds: claims backed by evidence, limits stated rather than glossed over, and a clear head about what is still unknown.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Hazard-driven thinking.&lt;/strong&gt; You start from what can go wrong and work forward to what would have to be tested to know — and you can work with the output of a hazard analysis rather than needing it translated for you.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Comfort with residual risk.&lt;/strong&gt; You can say what we do not know and how much of it we are accepting, without overstating it and without smoothing it away.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Influence without authority.&lt;/strong&gt; Much of this job is persuading engineers and QA colleagues to change how they work because your argument is good, not because you can require it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;A habit of checking the number yourself.&lt;/strong&gt; When a safety metric looks wrong, you go to the data behind it rather than accepting the dashboard.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Fluent use of LLMs as a working tool&lt;/strong&gt; — reading through standards, logs and results quickly, and drafting the documents this role produces.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Excellent written English.&lt;/strong&gt; Other people&#39;s decisions rest on your documents.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Nice to have&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Working knowledge of the standards that govern this work&lt;/strong&gt; — ISO 21448 (SOTIF), ISO/TS 5083, UL 4600, ISO 26262, ISO 34501–34505 — or their equivalents from your own field, such as DO-178C, IEC 62304 and ISO 14971, EN 50716, or IEC 61508. Whoever takes this role will become fluent in the AV-specific ones; we do not expect you to arrive that way.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Autonomous vehicles, ADAS, or another domain that operates without a human fallback.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Direct contribution to a safety case, a Voluntary Safety Self-Assessment, or a regulatory submission.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Experience defining on-road testing safety protocols and safety-driver procedures.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Experience hiring engineers.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Hands-on SQL and Python for digging into results yourself.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;&lt;div class=&quot;content-conclusion&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;em class=&quot;italic-text&quot;&gt;Candidates are required to be authorized to work in the U.S. The employer is not offering relocation, sponsorship, and remote work options are not available.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em class=&quot;italic-text&quot;&gt;Avride is an equal opportunity employer and committed to providing reasonable accommodations to qualified applicants and employees with disabilities to ensure they have equal access to employment opportunities. Avride complies with the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA), if you need a reasonable accommodation to assist with the application or hiring process, or to perform the essential functions of a job, please email &lt;a href=&quot;mailto:jobs@avride.ai&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot;&gt;jobs@avride.ai&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;h2 class=&quot;mt-3 -mb-1 text-[1.125rem] font-bold&quot; data-sourcepos=&quot;25:1-25:18;1601-1618&quot;&gt;About the role&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p class=&quot;font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal&quot; data-sourcepos=&quot;27:1-27:257;1620-1876&quot;&gt;We&#39;re hiring an experienced ML engineer to work on the models that see. You&#39;ll own problems end to end: deciding what data you need, getting it, training on it, proving the result is actually better, and getting it running inside the vehicle&#39;s constraints.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 class=&quot;mt-3 -mb-1 text-[1.125rem] font-bold&quot; data-sourcepos=&quot;29:1-29:36;1878-1913&quot;&gt;The problems you&#39;d be working on&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p class=&quot;font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal&quot; data-sourcepos=&quot;31:1-31:85;1915-1999&quot;&gt;Rather than a list of responsibilities, here&#39;s what the team is actually chewing on:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class=&quot;font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal&quot; data-sourcepos=&quot;33:1-33:338;2001-2338&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;A model that&#39;s two points better offline can be worse on the road.&lt;/strong&gt; Aggregate benchmark numbers hide the failures that matter — the rare scene, the unusual agent, the bad lighting. Building evaluation that predicts on-road behaviour, and knowing when to distrust your own metric, is a bigger part of this job than architecture search.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class=&quot;font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal&quot; data-sourcepos=&quot;35:1-35:290;2340-2629&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;We generate far more data than anyone can look at.&lt;/strong&gt; The interesting frames are a vanishingly small fraction of what the fleet records. Finding them, deciding what&#39;s worth labelling, and keeping the training set honest as the distribution shifts is continuous work, not a one-time setup.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class=&quot;font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal&quot; data-sourcepos=&quot;37:1-37:213;2631-2843&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The vehicle&#39;s compute budget is fixed and already full.&lt;/strong&gt; Everything you add competes with everything already running. You&#39;ll be making concrete trades between accuracy, latency, and memory, and defending them.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class=&quot;font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal&quot; data-sourcepos=&quot;39:1-39:295;2845-3139&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Modern architectures keep changing what&#39;s possible.&lt;/strong&gt; Transformers and multimodal models opened up approaches that weren&#39;t available two years ago. Part of the job is reading what&#39;s coming out, judging honestly whether it applies to our problem, and being willing to conclude that it doesn&#39;t.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class=&quot;font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal&quot; data-sourcepos=&quot;41:1-41:216;3141-3356&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Nothing ships alone.&lt;/strong&gt; Your model&#39;s output is someone else&#39;s input. You&#39;ll work directly with the planning, infrastructure, and vehicle software teams, and the handoffs are where most of the real difficulty lives.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 class=&quot;mt-3 -mb-1 text-[1.125rem] font-bold&quot; data-sourcepos=&quot;43:1-43:26;3358-3383&quot;&gt;What we&#39;re looking for&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;ul class=&quot;[li_&amp;amp;]:mb-0 [li_&amp;amp;]:mt-1 [li_&amp;amp;]:gap-1 [&amp;amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:pb-1 list-disc flex flex-col gap-1 pl-8 mb-3 print:block print:space-y-1&quot; data-sourcepos=&quot;45:1-51:138;3385-4396&quot;&gt;
&lt;li class=&quot;font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2&quot; data-sourcepos=&quot;45:1-45:211;3385-3595&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;You&#39;ve shipped a neural network, not just trained one.&lt;/strong&gt; At least three years taking models from data collection through training to something that ran in production or on real hardware, and stayed working.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li class=&quot;font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2&quot; data-sourcepos=&quot;46:1-46:176;3596-3771&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Real depth in one modern ML area&lt;/strong&gt; — computer vision, large language models, or generative modelling. We&#39;d rather see one domain you know properly than six you&#39;ve touched.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li class=&quot;font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2&quot; data-sourcepos=&quot;47:1-47:96;3772-3867&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Python and a modern deep learning framework&lt;/strong&gt;, fluently, as your daily working environment.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li class=&quot;font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2&quot; data-sourcepos=&quot;48:1-48:212;3868-4079&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Enough C++ to be useful.&lt;/strong&gt; Inference runs in C++ on the vehicle. You don&#39;t need to be a C++ specialist, but you need to be able to read the code your model runs inside and work with the engineers who own it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li class=&quot;font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2&quot; data-sourcepos=&quot;49:1-49:113;4080-4192&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Comfort with large-scale data tooling and SQL&lt;/strong&gt; — you can get your own data without waiting on someone else.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li class=&quot;font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2&quot; data-sourcepos=&quot;50:1-50:66;4193-4258&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;You read papers and can tell which ones matter.&lt;/strong&gt; Most don&#39;t.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li class=&quot;font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2&quot; data-sourcepos=&quot;51:1-51:138;4259-4396&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;You can explain a technical trade-off to someone who doesn&#39;t share your background&lt;/strong&gt; and hold your position when it&#39;s the right call.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h2 class=&quot;mt-3 -mb-1 text-[1.125rem] font-bold&quot; data-sourcepos=&quot;53:1-53:31;4398-4428&quot;&gt;Things that would stand out&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;ul class=&quot;[li_&amp;amp;]:mb-0 [li_&amp;amp;]:mt-1 [li_&amp;amp;]:gap-1 [&amp;amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:pb-1 list-disc flex flex-col gap-1 pl-8 mb-3 print:block print:space-y-1&quot; data-sourcepos=&quot;55:1-58:96;4430-4865&quot;&gt;
&lt;li class=&quot;font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2&quot; data-sourcepos=&quot;55:1-55:108;4430-4537&quot;&gt;You&#39;ve made a model meaningfully faster on target hardware and can explain what you gave up to get there.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li class=&quot;font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2&quot; data-sourcepos=&quot;56:1-56:129;4538-4666&quot;&gt;You&#39;ve worked on ML for autonomous vehicles or robotics before, and know how different the failure modes are from a benchmark.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li class=&quot;font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2&quot; data-sourcepos=&quot;57:1-57:103;4667-4769&quot;&gt;Published work or open-source contributions we can actually read — send us a link and we&#39;ll read it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li class=&quot;font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2&quot; data-sourcepos=&quot;58:1-58:96;4770-4865&quot;&gt;A track record of setting a direction and following it through without needing to be steered.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;ember-view setting-group-item promotion-settings-page__item&quot; data-test-selector=&quot;setting-group-item&quot;&gt;
